  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Snow showers and freezing drizzle continue in\nthe Alleghenies overnight. \n\nAn area of low pressure is now centered close to the NY/Quebec\nborder and will continue to track northeastward off toward the\nCanadian Maritimes overnight. Flow locally continues to be out\nof the west to southwest behind the departing low. While upslope\nflow into the Alleghenies isn't at the ideal angle (WSW as\nopposed to WNW to NW), the boundary layer is very moisture \nladen, with nearly saturated low-levels and low ceilings\nupstream across the Ohio Valley. As a result, on and off upslope\nsnow showers are expected to continue through much of the night\nin the Alleghenies. The forecast in terms of accumulations is a\ndifficult one, since there are a few complicating factors.\nFirst, surface temperatures are very marginal, especially below\n3000 ft. Currently temperatures are 32-34 at most locations\nbelow 3000 ft, so accumulations are somewhat dependent on\nsnowfall rates overcoming residual heat in the ground. The loss\nof daytime heating from the sun should help with the efficiency\nof accumulations, but some of the precipitation will still\nlikely be lost to melting, especially when it's lighter in\nnature. Second, model soundings suggest that there could be loss\nof ice crystals at times overnight as the subsidence inversion\nstarts to lower and saturation is lost at temperatures colder\nthan -5 to -7 C. This could lead to some of the snow turning \nover to freezing drizzle at times. For now, we're expecting a \ngeneral 1 to 3 inches of snow in the Alleghenies overnight, \nalong with a light glaze of ice. Isolated slightly higher totals\ncould be possible above 3000 feet. Winter Weather Advisories \nremain in effect for western Grant and western Pendleton \ncounties through 7 AM. Elsewhere, mostly cloudy skies are \nexpected overnight, with overnight lows in the 30s to lower 40s.\n\nAny snow showers or freezing drizzle in the Alleghenies should\ncome to an end shortly after daybreak tomorrow as the subsidence\ninversion further lowers. Residual clouds should tend to break\nup over the course of the day, leading to a fair amount of\nsunshine by tomorrow afternoon. Winds will be gusty out of the\nwest, but temperatures will run a bit warmer, with highs in the\nmid to upper 50s for most (30s/40s mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another potent area of low pressure brings precipitation\nand blustery conditions Sunday night into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure will keep the region dry Sunday. Temperatures will be \nnear seasonable with highs middle 50s to lower 60s. \n\nAs the high pushes further to the southeast Sunday night, a low \npressure system will move from the middle Mississippi Valley on \nSunday toward the eastern Great Lakes Sunday night. A trailing cold \nfront will push across the region Sunday night into Monday morning.\nShowers will likely accompany the front. We can't rule out a clap of \nthunder or two either given that there will be plenty of wind shear, \ndespite instability being minimal to nil and the time of night for \nfrontal passage. Highs cooler Sunday with highs mainly in lower to \nupper 50s.\n\nAs the front pushes through, winds will pick up substantially out of \nthe northwest on Monday in its wake. Wind gusts, especially along \nthe ridges out west, could approach wind advisory criteria. Areas \nalong the MD/PA border will also see gusts close to criteria, but \nnot very high confidence in the exact magnitude at this time. Still, \npotential for downed trees given the wet ground from the previous \nsystem. Otherwise, highs Monday reach the upper 40s to mid 50s \noutside of the mountains (they'll be in the 20s to 30s). Wind chills \nwill be quite cold on the ridges Monday, with values in the teens at \nthe highest elevations. \n\nOnce the front moves to the east and southeast Monday afternoon, \nhigh pressure will become reestablished and bring dry conditions and \nseasonable temperatures to the region through the middle of the \nweek.",
